  "prediction": "**Summary of the Report: Implementation of CAPTA Requirements for Substance-Affected Infants**\n\nThe report examines the implementation of the Child Abuse Prevention and Treatment Act (CAPTA) requirements for substance-affected infants. CAPTA requires states to have policies and procedures in place to address the needs of infants born with substance exposure or withdrawal symptoms. The report highlights the challenges states face in implementing these requirements, including a lack of clarity on key terms such as \"substance-affected\" and \"plan of safe care.\"\n\n**Key Findings:**\n\n* 46 states reported having policies and procedures for deciding which notifications about substance-affected infants are accepted for investigation.\n* 49 states reported developing plans of safe care for substance-affected infants who meet the state's criteria for investigation.\n* States reported challenges in understanding how to define \"substance-affected\" under CAPTA and how to develop plans of safe care.\n* Many states reported wanting more help from the Administration for Children and Families (ACF) to overcome their challenges, including trainings and teleconferences with other states.\n\n**Recommendation:**\n\nThe Government Accountability Office (GAO) recommends that the Secretary of Health and Human Services direct ACF to provide additional guidance and technical assistance to states to address known challenges and enhance their understanding of CAPTA requirements.\n\n**Challenges:**\n\n* States face challenges in implementing CAPTA requirements due to a lack of clarity on key terms.\n* States struggle to develop plans of safe care that meet the requirements of CAPTA.\n* The opioid epidemic has generated a significant increase in the number of substance-affected infants, placing a strain on state resources.\n\n**Next Steps:**\n\n* ACF will review state plans and provide technical assistance to states as needed.\n* The National Center on Substance Abuse and Child Welfare (NCSACW) will continue to offer technical assistance to states on the development and implementation of plans of safe care.\n\nOverall, the report highlights the need for additional guidance and technical assistance to help states implement CAPTA requirements and provide effective protections and services for substance-affected infants and their families.",
